---
import PageLayout from '../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import { SITE } from '../site.config';
---

<PageLayout
  title="Editorial Policy"
  subtitle="How we research, write, check and update every post"
  description={`How ${SITE.name} sources facts, reviews posts, keeps them updated and corrects mistakes.`}
  updated="3 October 2026"
>
  <p>
    Students make real decisions based on what we publish: which exam to register for, which deadline to chase,
    which program to spend a weekend on. This page explains the standards we follow so you know how much you can
    rely on our posts.
  </p>

  <h2>1. Where our facts come from</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Government jobs and exams:</strong> the official notification PDF or the official website of the recruiting body (for example upsc.gov.in, ssc.gov.in, the organising IIT's GATE site, or the PSU's careers page).</li>
    <li><strong>Government schemes:</strong> the official scheme portal, the ministry's notification, a PIB press release, or the state government's official website. We never cover a scheme that only appears in social media forwards.</li>
    <li><strong>Tech news:</strong> the company's own announcement, official blog, product page, or the government/regulator's official release.</li>
    <li><strong>Cinema:</strong> the production house's or distributor's official announcement and verified social accounts, the OTT platform's own page or press release, CBFC certification details, and official award announcements. Box office figures are reported as claimed by the makers or trade sources, and we say so. We never link to piracy sites or leaked content.</li>
    <li><strong>Company programs:</strong> the company's own careers or student-program page.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Every post links its main official source. News reports, social media posts and other blogs may help us
    discover a topic, but we never treat them as the source of a fact, and we never copy their text or images.
  </p>

  <h2>2. How a post is made</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>We read the official source fully and note the key facts: dates, eligibility, fees, prizes, selection process and how to apply.</li>
    <li>We write the post around those facts, adding practical advice: who it affects, what to do next, and common mistakes to avoid.</li>
    <li>Before publishing, every number and date is checked again against the official source, and anything we can't confirm is removed or clearly marked as not yet announced.</li>
    <li>We check each post for clarity, structure and working links before it goes live.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>3. Opinions and advice</h2>
  <p>
    Sections such as "Should you apply?" or "Our take" are our honest opinion based on the facts, past years and
    student experience. They're clearly written as opinion. They are not guarantees about selection, results or
    earnings.
  </p>

  <h2>4. Earning and side-income content</h2>
  <p>Any post about earning money, stipends or side income is held to a stricter standard:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Only realistic income ranges, with the effort and skills they need.</li>
    <li>No earning apps, task or survey apps, referral chains, betting, fantasy games, crypto or forex trading tips.</li>
    <li>No "guaranteed income" claims and no income screenshots.</li>
    <li>We say who an option is <em>not</em> suitable for.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>5. Updates</h2>
  <p>
    Deadlines get extended, exam dates change and results are declared. When that happens we update the post, add
    a short note near the top explaining what changed, and show the "Updated" date under the title.
  </p>

  <h2>6. Corrections</h2>
  <p>
    If we get something wrong, we fix it as soon as we've verified the correct information. Significant
    corrections are noted in the post. To report an error, email <a href={`mailto:${SITE.email}`}>{SITE.email}</a>
    {' '}with the post link and the official source.
  </p>

  <h2>7. Independence and advertising</h2>
  <p>
    We may earn money through display advertising (such as Google AdSense). Advertisers have no say in what we
    write. We don't accept payment to cover an opportunity or to give it a positive review. If we ever publish
    sponsored content or use affiliate links, we will label them clearly at the top of the post.
  </p>

  <h2>8. Questions</h2>
  <p>Questions about this policy? Write to <a href={`mailto:${SITE.email}`}>{SITE.email}</a>.</p>
</PageLayout>
